{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-137-144", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-01-governor-felix", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 20.137: Claudius sent Felix, the brother of Pallas, to take care of the affairs of Judea, in Claudius's twelfth year (52 CE); 'While Felix was procurator of Judea' (20.141) - the same person and office as Acts 24:1's 'the governor'."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-12-54", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-01-governor-felix", "grade": "A", "note": "Tacitus names 'his brother, surnamed Felix, who had for some time been governor of Judaea' - the same person and office, independent of Josephus and Acts."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-histories-5-9", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-01-governor-felix", "grade": "A", "note": "Tacitus: Claudius 'entrusted the province of Judæa to the Roman Knights or to his own freedmen, one of whom, Antonius Felix' - the same person and office."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-137-144", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-24-drusilla-jewess", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 20.141-144: Agrippa gave his sister Drusilla in marriage to Azizus king of Emesa, 'upon his consent to be circumcised'; while Felix was procurator she was 'prevailed upon to transgress the laws of her forefathers, and to marry Felix' - a Jewish princess, the Drusilla of Acts 24:24. Regraded A to B (reviewer 11, architect's titles/offices ruling): the Josephus evidence is A for Drusilla's person and marriage, now anchored on claim:ac-24-24-drusilla-person; it is B for the hearing event."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-182", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-27-festus-succeeded", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 20.182: 'Now when Porcius Festus was sent as successor to Felix by Nero' - names the same person, office and succession."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-182", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-27-felix-recalled", "grade": "B", "note": "The same passage: 'the principal of the Jewish inhabitants of Cesarea went up to Rome to accuse Felix; and he had certainly been brought to punishment, unless Nero had yielded to the importunate solicitations of his brother Pallas.' B: Josephus is the sole direct source and writes partly to defend the Herodian circle."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-12-54", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-10-judge-many-years", "grade": "B", "note": "'who had for some time been governor of Judaea' - confirms Felix had governed for years, though Tacitus gives no span."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-137-144", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-10-judge-many-years", "grade": "B", "note": "Claudius sent Felix 'when he had already completed the twelfth year of his reign' (52 CE); with the commonly given end c. 59/60 the tenure was about seven or eight years - 'many years' by the hearing."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-169-172", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-05-impostors-felix-era", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 20.167-172: impostors 'persuaded the multitude to follow them into the wilderness' and 'Felix brought them back, and then punished them'; the Egyptian prophet led crowds to the Mount of Olives and Felix attacked with cavalry - the same reign of seditious impostors the charge in 24:5 alludes to."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-169-172", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-05-insurrection-charge", "grade": "C", "note": "Does not attest the charge against Paul; shows the period's epidemic of seditious impostors and staseis 'among all the Jews' that made Tertullus's charge plausible."}
{"from": "text:tertullian-adv-marcion-4-8", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-05-nazarenes", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception: Tertullian records that 'the Jews called Christians Nazarenes' from Jesus being a man of Nazareth - a tradition independent of Acts' wording but two generations later; dependence and distance noted."}
{"from": "text:birkat-haminim-nazarenes", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-05-nazarenes", "grade": "D", "note": "The Cairo Geniza version of the Birkat ha-Minim adds 'the Nazarenes' (ha-nazarim/notzrim) to the curse on the minim - but the date of the addition is disputed (anywhere from before Yavneh to the 4th century), so D."}
{"from": "artifact:felix-procurator-prutah", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-01-governor-felix", "grade": "B", "note": "A bronze prutah minted under Felix's administration carries the emperor's name (Nero/'Caesar'), not Felix's - it confirms Roman procuratorial rule of Judaea in his years, not Felix's personal name."}
{"from": "artifact:festus-procurator-prutah", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-27-festus-succeeded", "grade": "B", "note": "A prutah minted by Festus dated year 5 of Nero (58/59) with the emperor's name - confirms Festus was procurator of Judaea by 58/59, per the fetched caption."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-histories-5-9", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-26-bribe-hope", "grade": "C", "note": "Tacitus's portrait: Felix 'indulging in every kind of barbarity and lust, exercised the power of a king in the spirit of a slave' - the venal, base exercise of power Acts attributes to him; it supports the character trait, not the specific bribe attempt of 24:26."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-182", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-24-27-two-years", "grade": "B", "note": "Attests the succession event only ('Porcius Festus was sent as successor to Felix by Nero'); Josephus gives no date, so the two-year chronology's absolute date stays disputed (D) - see the claim note. Regraded A to B by the architect: Josephus names the succession, which is A on claim:ac-24-27-festus-succeeded, but not the two years, so A cannot cover this claim."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-histories-5-9", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-24-24-drusilla-jewess", "note": "Tacitus calls Drusilla 'the granddaughter of Antony and Cleopatra' and Felix 'the grandson-in-law' of Antony (so a pagan princess) - a different identification from Josephus's Jewish Agrippid princess, and contrary to Luke's 'a Jewess'."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-histories-5-9", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-24-02-03-flattery", "note": "Tacitus records 'every kind of barbarity and lust' under Felix - the opposite of the 'much peace' and prosperity Tertullus's oratory attributes to him."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-169-172", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:ac-24-02-03-flattery", "note": "Josephus records impostors leading crowds into the wilderness, the Egyptian prophet, and Felix suppressing them by force - internal feuds and disturbances, not the peace Tertullus claims."}
